"""Keep recording identification in notes; use body paragraphs to teach biology."""
PATCH={
4:'The pressure transformation does not create energy. Increasing pressure is accompanied by a change in displacement and mechanical loading across the ossicular chain. Fluid motion then produces a pressure difference across the cochlear partition, allowing a small middle-ear movement to initiate a spatially organized wave within the inner ear.',
10:'The base-to-apex gradient means that damage at one cochlear region need not affect every frequency equally. Losing basal receptor function particularly disrupts access to higher-frequency information. Apical damage affects a different part of the frequency map, while a widespread injury can impair responses over a much broader range.',
12:'The reticular lamina and basilar membrane also respond differently when the living cochlea loses its active processes. Comparing living and postmortem measurements helps separate mechanically coupled passive motion from motion requiring functioning cells. The loss of sensitivity changes the internal vibration pattern rather than simply scaling every structure’s displacement equally.',
14:'A delay between two measured structures changes the relative shear available to the sensory bundle. Even when their displacement amplitudes are similar, different timing can produce relative movement. Following amplitude and phase together therefore gives a more complete account of the mechanical stimulus than measuring the maximum displacement alone.',
16:'The experiment used combinations of acoustic frequencies to examine nonlinear components of cochlear motion. A nonlinear system produces output components that are absent from a simple sum of the inputs. Tracking these components across frequency helped separate properties of the outer-hair-cell-related response from the imposed acoustic stimulus.',
18:'Prestin’s motor role is distinct from the mechanically sensitive channel at the bundle. The channel changes electrical current in response to force; prestin changes cell shape in response to voltage. Coupling these steps permits a mechanically driven receptor potential to feed energy back into the vibrating organ of Corti.',
20:'A single inner hair cell supplies several separate afferent contacts, allowing different fibers to sample its output through synapses with different properties. Outer hair cells have a much smaller afferent representation. Their large influence on hearing instead arises mainly from how they modify the mechanics experienced by nearby sensory receptors.',
24:'Releasing tip-link tension changes the bundle’s resting position and its stiffness. Measuring that displacement after disrupting the links allows investigators to estimate the mechanical contribution carried by the link-associated apparatus. The observed gradients therefore concern force transmission within the bundle, rather than simply a difference in the overall size of each cell.',
26:'This arrangement places the main entry and exit routes on different parts of a highly polarized cell. Current enters near the bundle and changes the voltage sensed by channels near the synaptic base. The receptor can thereby transform a small apical movement into altered transmitter output at a separate membrane region.',
28:'The study used nuclear magnetic resonance measurements to test interactions predicted for the protein complex. This method probes molecular environments and contacts within selected protein fragments. Combining measured binding with loss-of-function experiments strengthens the proposed assembly mechanism, while the exact full-channel architecture remains a structural model rather than directly observed motion.',
30:'Defective delivery reduces the machinery available to receive force at the bundle, so increasing sound pressure cannot supply the missing molecular component. A rescue experiment must restore the correct protein function or the delivery route. The biochemical resemblance between mTOMT and a related enzyme was insufficient to predict which replacement would work.',
32:'Only the stereocilia associated with transduction channels showed the characteristic shortening after current reduction. This selectivity argues against a uniform shrinkage of the whole bundle. The maintained electrical and calcium environment acts locally on projections with a particular sensory role, preserving the unequal heights required for directional mechanical detection.',
36:'Membrane filtering depends on the balance between membrane charge storage and the ion channels carrying current across it. A cell with faster electrical responses can preserve more of a low-frequency waveform’s alternating component. A slower response smooths rapid changes, altering the information presented to the voltage-dependent release machinery at the synaptic base.',
40:'The loss of both proteins reduced spontaneous and sound-evoked signaling by spiral ganglion neurons. Because the defect occurred in calcium-channel regulation, mechanical stimulation could reach the receptor yet fail to produce adequate sustained neural output. The result locates one form of hearing dysfunction between the hair-cell voltage response and the auditory nerve’s spikes.',
42:'The investigators used both presynaptic and postsynaptic electrical measurements to establish that the light stimulus produced effective transmission before analyzing the frozen tissue. A light-sensitive protein alone would not establish release. Recording the receiving bouton confirmed that activation of the receptor actually generated a transmitter-dependent response across the synaptic cleft.',
44:'The manipulation also altered calcium-channel organization, producing more spatially spread presynaptic calcium signals. Several small active zones did not recreate the normal concentration of release machinery around a ribbon. The reduced neural output therefore accompanied an altered arrangement of the calcium trigger and vesicle supply at the same contact.',
46:'The higher-spontaneous-release synapses also produced stronger initial responses to controlled depolarization. These differences can allow separate fibers attached to one receptor to contribute different ranges of output. The relevant diversity occurs at individual contacts, so averaging release across the whole hair cell would conceal part of the peripheral code.',
48:'A spike generated near the peripheral ending propagates along the neuron’s axon without reproducing the hair cell’s continuously varying voltage amplitude. The synapse converts one kind of signal into another: graded receptor depolarization changes release probability, while the nerve represents the resulting input through discrete electrical events.',
50:'Active outer-hair-cell mechanics sharpen the frequency range represented at each location, especially for quiet sounds. Damage to that amplification broadens the mechanical input and can reduce frequency selectivity in the nerve fibers receiving it. A place code therefore depends on both anatomical position and the quality of local mechanical filtering.',
52:'Different harmonics can overlap within a fiber’s tuning range, especially at stronger sound levels. The neural response then reflects both the incoming spectrum and the cochlea’s filtering. Changes in the source’s envelope can also alter synchrony across fibers even when the average distribution of spectral energy remains broadly similar.',
54:'The contacts with higher spontaneous release also had shorter synaptic delays and higher initial release rates during the controlled stimuli. These properties can change both sensitivity and onset precision. Separate synapses therefore differ in how quickly they respond as well as in how many transmitter-driven events they produce over time.',
60:'At an outer hair cell, efferent acetylcholine activates receptors that permit calcium entry and recruit calcium-sensitive potassium conductance. The resulting electrical change reduces the cell’s effective response to mechanical input. This local receptor mechanism can alter cochlear gain without requiring the outer hair cell or its sensory bundle to disappear.',
62:'The vulnerability of these components differs with injury type and time after exposure. A sensory cell can remain alive with a damaged bundle, and an intact bundle can coexist with a failed release site. Examining the surviving structures identifies whether recovery requires local repair, restoration of a molecular function, or replacement of a lost cell.',
64:'Synapse loss after overstimulation can develop alongside changes in the hair bundle and surrounding tissue. Following recovery at several times separates rapid mechanical or ionic improvement from slower restoration of contacts. The accessible fish organ allows these cellular measurements to be compared with behavior under the same defined injury condition.',
68:'A repair strategy must also consider whether a surviving auditory neuron still has a receptor to contact. Repairing a release site can help a living inner hair cell communicate, while replacing a missing cell must re-establish that interface. Loss of the neuron itself creates a further requirement that increasing acoustic level cannot resolve.'
}
def refine(content):
 for i,text in PATCH.items():
  content[i]['recording_context_note']=content[i]['body'][2]
  content[i]['body'][2]=text
 return content
